WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.120
House of the Dragon has plenty of dark scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.140
but this part of Episode 7 may be the&nbsp;
darkest – literally. And that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.280
least controversial thing about this season&nbsp;
that has fans up in arms. Spoilers ahead!

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.120
House of the Dragon Episode 1, "The&nbsp;
Heirs of the Dragon," revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.380
a tournament heralding the birth of the&nbsp;
new prince. Viserys is sure it'll be a boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.260
while Queen Aemma just wants an&nbsp;
end to her miserable pregnancy.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.580
Her labor is intercut with the tourney.&nbsp;
The knights smash each other's faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.440
but the real horror unfolds in Aemma's bedroom.&nbsp;
The maesters tell Viserys that they cannot save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.060
Aemma, but they may yet save the child. It's&nbsp;
upsetting enough that Viserys is given control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.160
over Aemma's body. It's worse still that he can't&nbsp;
bring himself to tell her the truth. Aemma suffers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.720
excruciating pain and panic as the maesters&nbsp;
not-so-carefully slice her open and pull the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.820
infant from her womb. She dies moments later, and&nbsp;
baby Baelon perishes before the day is through.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.080
Some viewers felt the episode should've had a&nbsp;
trigger warning, and many lamented that the Game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.680
of Thrones universe was still torturing&nbsp;
and disposing of women for shock value.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.480
We meet Daemon's mistress, Mysaria, in&nbsp;
"The Heirs of the Dragon," then get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.500
know her better in Episode 2, "The Rogue&nbsp;
Prince." Daemon steals a dragon egg and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.000
takes off for Dragonstone on Caraxes&nbsp;
with the supposedly pregnant Mysaria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.460
Otto and the Kingsguard arrive&nbsp;
to negotiate his surrender.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.940
"Welcome to Dragonstone, Otto."

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.800
But Rhaenyra and Syrax have everything&nbsp;
under control. She lands her dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.300
de-escalates the situation, and recovers&nbsp;
the egg. It turns out Mysaria isn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.600
with child, and she's not happy to&nbsp;
have been used in Daemon's scheme.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.980
"I have been sold as property&nbsp;
more times than I care to count.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.180
Beginning in a homeland I can no longer recall."

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.240
Viewers were fine with the story. They&nbsp;
just couldn't wrap their heads around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.580
Mysaria's accent. As Fire &amp; Blood details,&nbsp;
Mysaria is a dancer from Lys who's smarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.900
and more capable than most people assume.&nbsp;
But the choice of accent exoticizes her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.640
and is simply difficult to understand. No one&nbsp;
else from this region of George R.R. Martin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.380
world sounds this way. Nor does actress Sonoya&nbsp;
Mizuno, who was raised in Somerset, England.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.440
As if pimping out his daughter weren't bad enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.280
Otto Hightower takes things even further&nbsp;
in Episode 3, "Second of his Name."

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.840
As soon as Alicent gives birth to little&nbsp;
Aegon, Otto begins scheming to put his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.920
grandson on the Iron Throne. This episode&nbsp;
follows a hunt held to celebrate the boy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.660
second birthday. The Hand uses the opportunity&nbsp;
to sway Viserys with tradition and symbols.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.380
"I've never been one for signs&nbsp;
and portents, your Grace, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.720
if the gods did wish to show their favor."

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.240
But when he doesn't seem to be getting anywhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.840
he changes tactics. Princess Rhaenyra&nbsp;
is still in need of a husband.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.940
"You must marry."

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.400
When Viserys vents about the&nbsp;
political problems this presents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.000
Otto suggests that perhaps the two-year-old&nbsp;
Prince Aegon and the 17-year-old Princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.620
Rhaenyra could marry each other. The Targaryen&nbsp;
king, with his long family history of incest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.520
balks at the idea. Besides, even&nbsp;
though he's 15 years her junior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.600
we wouldn't want to inflict Aegon's&nbsp;
depravity and abuse on poor Rhaenyra.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.740
Episode 4 of House of the Dragon, "King of&nbsp;
the Narrow Sea," is among the most salacious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.360
hours of television set in Westeros so far.&nbsp;
Rhaenyra very nearly hooks up with her uncle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.680
Daemon before she coerces her employee&nbsp;
and sworn protector, Ser Criston Cole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.940
into bed. Then, she swears on her dead mother&nbsp;
that what we just saw happen didn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:20.629
happen. But for all Rhaenyra's wildness, viewers&nbsp;
found another sex scene harder to stomach.

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:23.280
Alicent is settling in for the night&nbsp;
when a servant interrupts her peace.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.920
"Pardon me, Your Grace. The king&nbsp;
has requested your presence."

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.680
This is an order and not an offer. As&nbsp;
Rhaenyra is enjoying sowing her oats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.580
Alicent lies still and expressionless&nbsp;
underneath the king, who is, as fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.020
pointed out, visibly ill. Not even the queen&nbsp;
of the Seven Kingdoms rules her own body.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.000
Otto Hightower might be the force behind the&nbsp;
Greens' treachery, but Ser Criston Cole — who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.320
carries out most of Hightower's schemes — might go&nbsp;
down as the most hated character on House of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.120
Dragon. After their dalliance, Criston asks&nbsp;
Rhaenyra to run away with him. She refuses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.380
but doesn't exactly reject him, merely&nbsp;
explaining she must fulfill her duties as heir.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:09.300
"I am the crown, Ser Criston. Or I will be."

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.820
It soon becomes clear that Criston never loved&nbsp;
Rhaenyra. He's an insecure, possessive hypocrite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:19.740
with the shortest-ever fuse and the longest-ever&nbsp;
grudges, which makes fans absolutely loathe him.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.420
At the feast to kick off Rhaenyra's wedding to&nbsp;
Laenor Velaryon, the groom's not-entirely-secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.980
boyfriend, Joffrey, susses out that the princess&nbsp;
and her sworn protector must be sleeping together.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.409
"I know who it is."
"Who?"&nbsp;

00:04:32.409 --> 00:04:35.700
"The handsome paramour."

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.400
Joffrey doesn't realize their&nbsp;
relationship is past tense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.360
so when he approaches Ser Criston to form an&nbsp;
alliance, he's tragically unprepared for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.980
knight's extreme overreaction. As the gathered&nbsp;
wedding guests take to dancing, Criston knocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:50.880
Joffrey to the floor, then beats him until&nbsp;
his face is pulverized beyond recognition.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.900
We don't know what, precisely, Criston's&nbsp;
motivation is. He might be worried word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.520
of his broken vows of chastity will get out, or&nbsp;
perhaps he's simply spiteful. Whatever the reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.960
fans find this vicious murder abominable.&nbsp;
Joffrey and Laenor appear to have a healthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.920
loving partnership. To many, including&nbsp;
The Advocate, Joffrey's murder feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.880
like a particularly egregious example of&nbsp;
media's tendency to kill off gay characters.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.640
Healthy relationships are rare in Westeros, so&nbsp;
when fans miss an opportunity to see one unfold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:21.720
they get upset. Harwin Strong doesn't&nbsp;
get many lines prior to Episode 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.840
"The Princess and the Queen."&nbsp;
Non-book readers may have entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.620
missed him admiring Rhaenyra at the hunt&nbsp;
or bumping into her on the Street of Silk.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.620
"And who might you be running from now?"&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.620
"Sir Harwin."
"Princess."

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.500
But after the 10-year time jump, we can&nbsp;
tell from pretty obvious context clues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.220
that Harwin and Rhaenyra are very fond of&nbsp;
each other and have had a relationship for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.460
most of the previous decade. He's&nbsp;
the real father of her three boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:50.940
and is as doting a family man as&nbsp;
he can be under the circumstances.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.980
He tells his own father in response to rumors,&nbsp;
"You have your honor and I have mine." That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.540
honor compels him to defend Rhaenyra and her sons&nbsp;
against Criston and his insults by way of a good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:06.000
strong punch. Unfortunately, this costs him&nbsp;
his job, and, soon after, his life. When they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.320
say their goodbyes, he and Rhaenyra make eyes at&nbsp;
each other, but alas, "Breakbones," as he's known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.120
must leave the princess and princes without so&nbsp;
much as a goodbye kiss. This irritated fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.500
who might have liked a positive scene of physical&nbsp;
contact after so many portrayals of assault and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:24.060
abuse. Failing that, fans of Harwin just wish&nbsp;
he could've stuck around a little longer.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.800
Episode 7 of Season 1, "Driftmark," sent&nbsp;
fans into a tizzy. But they weren't upset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.780
by violence or cruelty — they were bothered by&nbsp;
the lighting. "Driftmark" opens with Laena's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:37.680
twilit funeral. After the sun goes down,&nbsp;
Daemon and Rhaenyra consummate their 15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.640
years of flirtation on the beach, while&nbsp;
Aemond sneaks out to test drive Vhagar.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.920
"Vhagar is my mother's dragon."&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.100
"Your mother's dead. And&nbsp;
Vhagar has a new rider now."

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.340
In the aftermath, Aemond instigates&nbsp;
a brawl and loses an eye.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.460
House of the Dragon fans complained that&nbsp;
they couldn't see what was going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:58.020
due to the episode's extremely dim lighting.&nbsp;
Many compared the installment to the infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:02.100
Season 8 Game of Thrones episode "The&nbsp;
Long Night." The long-awaited Battle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.400
Winterfell underwhelmed audiences,&nbsp;
despite its record-breaking scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.660
because everything was so darn dark. When fans&nbsp;
expressed their outrage that this happened again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:13.440
with "Driftmark," the show defended the&nbsp;
lighting as a purposeful artistic choice.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.700
Since House of the Dragon takes place in a&nbsp;
world without electricity, a certain dimness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:22.620
is understandable. But many fans believe this nod&nbsp;
to authenticity interrupts basic storytelling.
 &nbsp;

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:27.420
While Daemon, Criston, and even Otto age&nbsp;
well from episode to episode, King Viserys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.620
looks more and more worse for the wear. But to&nbsp;
everyone's surprise, he is at his most heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:36.180
when he's at his most decrepit in Episode 8,&nbsp;
"The Lord of the Tides." George R. R. Martin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.500
told actor Paddy Considine that he prefers his&nbsp;
take on the character to his own, comparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:45.780
Considine's Viserys to King Lear. Fans agreed,&nbsp;
championing Viserys' last stand for his daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:50.520
and lamenting his final family dinner. Then comes&nbsp;
the misunderstanding to end all misunderstandings.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:54.180
With his final breath, the withered king&nbsp;
thinks he's continuing a life-or-death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.920
conversation with his daughter, when really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:00.060
he's talking to his wife. He means to tell&nbsp;
Rhaenyra that he believes in Aegon's dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.840
and, what's more, he believes the Prince&nbsp;
That Was Promised will come from her line.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.700
"To unite the realm. Against&nbsp;
the cold. And the dark."

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:13.020
But Alicent wrongfully interprets these&nbsp;
phrases to mean that he wants their son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.400
Aegon to succeed him. Instead of setting&nbsp;
things right, Viserys messes everything up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:21.420
just when he was finally ruling the realm and&nbsp;
his family with strength and solidity. Book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:26.100
readers were particularly taken aback by this&nbsp;
development. In Fire &amp; Blood, Otto and Alicent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:30.420
usurp the throne just because they want to.&nbsp;
This show-invented ending completely changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:35.280
the reason the Dance of the Dragons starts at all,&nbsp;
which many fans find unnecessary and frustrating.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.240
The drama that's been building up all&nbsp;
season comes to a head in Episode 9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:43.800
"The Green Council," when the Greens seize&nbsp;
power. It takes a lot of objectionable behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.780
to pull off this coup. Criston brutally kills&nbsp;
Lord Beesbury during a small council meeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:53.040
Otto seeks to assassinate the other half of&nbsp;
Viserys' family. The servants are imprisoned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.840
and Rhaenys is kept captive in her room. We&nbsp;
find out that Aegon II not only has myriad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.620
bastards running around Flea Bottom, he&nbsp;
spends his spare time at child fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.880
pits. Oh, and Meleys kills hundreds of&nbsp;
commoners while escaping the Dragonpit.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.680
But what really upsets viewers is the price&nbsp;
Larys puts on his information. Alicent's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.640
personal Master of Whisperers approaches her&nbsp;
with some intel concerning her father. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.600
he spills the beans, he wants something&nbsp;
in return. Alicent takes off her shoes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.680
When that doesn't produce the desired&nbsp;
result, she removes her stockings. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.280
all happens without a word being spoken&nbsp;
between them, which is proof enough that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:30.360
it's happened before. She turns her head as&nbsp;
Larys enjoys the sight of her naked feet.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.500
Larys is exploiting Alicent, and Alicent&nbsp;
knows it — but there's nothing she can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.540
do. This squirmy scene is vintage Game of&nbsp;
Thrones: It's out of left field, gross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.500
and meant to shock us. Indeed, fans couldn't&nbsp;
stop talking about this moment after "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:48.240
Green Council" aired. Even diehard Rhaenyra fans&nbsp;
can't help but feel for the queen in this moment.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.200
Twins Jaime and Cersei Lannisters'&nbsp;
long-term relationship is at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.260
heart of their dysfunction on Game&nbsp;
of Thrones. But both the creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.040
and fans of House of the Dragon are more on&nbsp;
board with Daemon and Rhaenyra's romance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:03.000
though they're uncle and niece. Maybe the&nbsp;
fact that it's a Targaryen tradition makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:07.440
them okay with it. Maybe it's just that Matt&nbsp;
Smith has chemistry with everybody. Maybe it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:11.460
that once they exchange their very particular&nbsp;
marriage vows, Daemon and Rhaenyra seem pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.600
happy together. Whatever the reason, Season&nbsp;
1 got many to root for this uncommon couple.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:19.860
So viewers were understandably shaken by the&nbsp;
moment Daemon grabs his wife by the throat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:25.200
in Episode 10, "The Black Queen." Rhaenyra is&nbsp;
considering Alicent's terms. She feels Viserys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.440
tasked her with preparing for the coming war&nbsp;
in the North and holding the realm together;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:32.340
whether she sits on the Iron Throne&nbsp;
while doing that is of less consequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:37.140
She confides this in Daemon, thinking he's&nbsp;
been let in on the secret of Aegon's dream.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:43.320
"This secret has been passed from&nbsp;
king to heir since Aegon's time."

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:47.460
But he hasn't. Daemon seems to become&nbsp;
enraged by the thought that Rhaenyra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:51.240
might be a cautious and omen-savvy&nbsp;
ruler, like her father was. He grows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.300
even angrier when he realizes Viserys&nbsp;
didn't trust him with this information.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:59.640
"Daemon was not made to wear the crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.140
but I believe that you were."

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.020
Though we've seen Daemon react&nbsp;
impulsively on several occasions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:08.400
many viewers felt this abuse was out of&nbsp;
character. Notably, this scene doesn't occur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.780
in Fire &amp; Blood. Daemon isn't a hero and isn't&nbsp;
even a good husband to Rhaenyra in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.380
but this moment still felt like a&nbsp;
bridge too far to plenty of fans.

